const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Clinical evaluation report","serviceType":"Regulatory writing service","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/medical-writing/regulatory-writing/clinical-evaluation-report/","description":"Clinical evaluation plans and reports written to EU MDR Annex XIV and MEDDEV 2.7/1 Rev 4.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Who is qualified to write a clinical evaluation?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"MDR Annex XIV requires the clinical evaluator’s qualifications to be documented and justified, and Notified Bodies read the CV. In practice that means a relevant degree, documented experience in the device area and in clinical research methodology, and a written justification specific to that device.\n\nIt is not a licensed role — there is no register to be on — but it is a real bar, and it is the requirement manufacturers most often discover at submission. We supply the named evaluator, the CV, the declaration of interests and the written justification as part of the deliverable."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How often does a CER have to be updated?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"MDR requires the clinical evaluation to be updated throughout the device lifecycle. For Class III and implantable devices the PMCF evaluation report is updated at least annually; for other classes the cadence is set by risk and justified in the plan, commonly every two to five years.\n\nThe practical answer is that it should also be updated whenever the state of the art moves, when PMCF data arrives, or when a safety signal appears — and the plan should say which of those triggers an update rather than leaving it to judgement."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can we still use equivalence under MDR?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, but the bar is considerably higher than under the Directive. MDCG 2020-5 requires technical, biological and clinical equivalence to be demonstrated separately, and for implantable and Class III devices the manufacturer must have a contract giving sufficient access to the equivalent device’s technical documentation.\n\nThat data-access requirement is where most equivalence arguments fail. We assess it early, because if it cannot be met the evaluation route changes and everything downstream changes with it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What exactly is the state of the art, and why is it separate?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It is what current practice achieves for the indication — from clinical guidelines, systematic reviews, comparable devices and alternative therapies — including typical performance and known complication rates.\n\nIt is separate because it is the benchmark your device is measured against. Folded into your own evidence it stops being a comparison, and reviewers look for it specifically. For legacy devices under MDCG 2020-6 it is central to whether the clinical evidence is sufficient at all."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Our report cites ISO 14155:2020. Is that a problem?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It cites a withdrawn standard. The fourth edition, ISO 14155:2026, was published on 23 March 2026 and replaced the 2020 version.\n\nOn its own it is not a safety issue, but it tells a reviewer when the file was last genuinely reviewed, and that shapes how closely the rest is read. It is also trivial to fix, which is why leaving it is worse than it looks."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you write the PMCF plan and report as well?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and they belong together. The CER identifies the residual gaps; the PMCF plan says how they will be closed; the PMCF evaluation report closes them and feeds the next CER update.\n\nBuying them apart means the gaps identified in one document are not the gaps addressed in the next, which is a finding in itself. For Class III and implantable devices this loop runs annually."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you write the SSCP too?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. The Summary of Safety and Clinical Performance is mandatory for Class III and implantable devices under MDCG 2019-9 Rev 1, and it has to be written for patients as well as for healthcare professionals.\n\nThat dual register is the hard part — the patient section has to be accurate without being impenetrable, which is the same skill as a plain language summary rather than a regulatory writing skill."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Are you a Notified Body, or can you audit us?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, and we could not be both. Notified Bodies are accredited to assess, and a firm that writes your clinical evaluation cannot also assess it.\n\nWe author the plan and the report; you review, approve and sign them; you remain the manufacturer and carry the responsibility. That separation is what keeps the document usable."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Regulatory writing","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/medical-writing/regulatory-writing/clinical-evaluation-report/"}]}];

